Task 0: Establish a Performance Baseline
Before changing anything, you must capture the cluster's current state. Every optimization claim later in this lab is only meaningful if compared against a baseline you actually measured.

Subtask 0.1: Build and Run the Hotspot Monitor Script
Connect to the first monitor node:
ssh ceph-mon-01
Create the hotspot monitoring dashboard script. This script reports cluster health, OSD utilization variance, utilization extremes, PG distribution, and slow-operation counts.
cat > ~/hotspot-monitor.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "Ceph Hotspot Monitoring Dashboard"
echo "================================="
echo "Timestamp: $(date)"
echo ""

# Cluster health overview
echo "1. Cluster Health:"
echo "-----------------"
sudo ceph health
echo ""

# OSD utilization variance
echo "2. OSD Utilization Variance:"
echo "---------------------------"
sudo ceph osd df | awk 'NR>1 && $8 ~ /^[0-9.]+$/ {
    util[count] = $8
    total += $8
    count++
}
END {
    if (count == 0) { print "No OSD utilization data available"; exit }
    avg = total/count
    variance = 0
    for (i=0; i<count; i++) {
        variance += (util[i] - avg)^2
    }
    variance = variance/count
    stddev = sqrt(variance)
    cv = (avg > 0) ? stddev/avg * 100 : 0

    print "Average utilization: " avg
    print "Standard deviation: " stddev
    print "Coefficient of variation: " cv "%"
    if (cv < 10) print "Status: GOOD - Low variance"
    else if (cv < 20) print "Status: MODERATE - Some imbalance"
    else print "Status: HIGH - Significant hotspots"
}'
echo ""

# Top and bottom OSDs by utilization
echo "3. OSD Utilization Extremes:"
echo "---------------------------"
echo "Highest utilized OSDs:"
sudo ceph osd df | awk 'NR>1 {print $1, $8}' | sort -k2 -nr | head -3 | awk '{print "  OSD." $1 ": " $2 "x average"}'
echo "Lowest utilized OSDs:"
sudo ceph osd df | awk 'NR>1 {print $1, $8}' | sort -k2 -n | head -3 | awk '{print "  OSD." $1 ": " $2 "x average"}'
echo ""

# PG distribution analysis
echo "4. PG Distribution Analysis:"
echo "---------------------------"
sudo ceph pg dump osds 2>/dev/null | awk 'NR>1 && $2 ~ /^[0-9]+$/ {print $2}' | sort -n | awk '
{
    pgs[NR] = $1
    total += $1
    count++
}
END {
    if (count == 0) { print "No PG data available"; exit }
    avg = total/count
    min = pgs[1]
    max = pgs[count]
    range = max - min

    print "Average PGs per OSD: " avg
    print "Min PGs: " min ", Max PGs: " max
    print "Range: " range " (" int(range/avg*100) "% of average)"

    if (range/avg < 0.1) print "PG Distribution: EXCELLENT"
    else if (range/avg < 0.2) print "PG Distribution: GOOD"
    else if (range/avg < 0.3) print "PG Distribution: MODERATE"
    else print "PG Distribution: NEEDS IMPROVEMENT"
}'
echo ""

# Recent slow operations
echo "5. Performance Issues:"
echo "---------------------"
slow_ops=$(sudo ceph health detail | grep -c "slow")
if [ "$slow_ops" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "WARNING: $slow_ops slow operation warnings detected"
    sudo ceph health detail | grep slow | head -5
else
    echo "No slow operations detected"
fi
EOF

chmod +x ~/hotspot-monitor.sh
Run the script and save its output as your baseline. This baseline file is the reference you will compare against at the end of the lab.
./hotspot-monitor.sh | tee ~/baseline-metrics.txt
Deliverable: A file ~/baseline-metrics.txt containing real, current cluster metrics (coefficient of variation, PG distribution, slow-op count). Confirm it is non-empty with cat ~/baseline-metrics.txt â�� you should see actual numbers pulled from your cluster, not placeholder text.

Task 1: Tune CRUSH Map and OSD Parameters for Performance
Subtask 1.1: Analyze Current CRUSH Map Configuration
Check overall cluster health and status:
sudo ceph health detail
sudo ceph -s
Examine the current CRUSH map:
sudo ceph osd getcrushmap -o crushmap.bin
sudo crushtool -d crushmap.bin -o crushmap.txt
cat crushmap.txt
Analyze CRUSH rule configuration:
sudo ceph osd crush rule ls
sudo ceph osd crush rule dump replicated_rule
Deliverable: A saved crushmap.txt file and command output showing the existing rule set (rule name, min_size, max_size, and steps). You should be able to state, in one sentence, which failure domain (host, chassis, rack, etc.) the default rule uses.

Subtask 1.2: Optimize CRUSH Map and OSD Parameters
Back up the current CRUSH map before making any changes:
mkdir -p ~/ceph-backups
sudo ceph osd getcrushmap -o ~/ceph-backups/crushmap-original.bin
Create a performance-optimized CRUSH rule that targets hdd-class devices explicitly (useful when you want to separate bulk HDD traffic from a future SSD pool):
sudo ceph osd crush rule create-replicated performance_rule default host hdd

sudo ceph osd crush rule ls
sudo ceph osd crush rule dump performance_rule
Tune OSD operation queue and thread settings to improve I/O scheduling fairness and parallelism:
# Check current values first
sudo ceph config get osd osd_op_queue
sudo ceph config get osd osd_max_backfills
sudo ceph config get osd osd_recovery_max_active

# Apply performance-oriented settings
sudo ceph config set osd osd_op_queue wpq
sudo ceph config set osd osd_op_queue_cut_off high
sudo ceph config set osd osd_op_num_threads_per_shard 2
sudo ceph config set osd osd_op_num_shards 8
Tune recovery/backfill throttling so background rebalancing does not starve client I/O:
sudo ceph config set osd osd_recovery_max_active 3
sudo ceph config set osd osd_max_backfills 1
sudo ceph config set osd osd_recovery_sleep 0.1
sudo ceph config set osd osd_recovery_sleep_hdd 0.1
Tune BlueStore cache and allocation size based on the media type:
sudo ceph config set osd bluestore_cache_size_hdd 1073741824   # 1GB for HDD
sudo ceph config set osd bluestore_cache_size_ssd 3221225472   # 3GB for SSD
sudo ceph config set osd bluestore_min_alloc_size_hdd 65536    # 64KB for HDD
sudo ceph config set osd bluestore_min_alloc_size_ssd 16384    # 16KB for SSD
Verify the settings were applied:
sudo ceph config dump | grep -E "osd_op_queue|osd_max_backfills|osd_recovery_max_active|bluestore_cache_size|bluestore_min_alloc_size"
Deliverable: Command output confirming every parameter set in steps 3-5 now shows the new value in ceph config dump. If any value does not match what you set, re-run the corresponding ceph config set command and re-verify.

Subtask 1.3: Monitor Cluster Impact of the Changes
sudo ceph -s
sudo ceph pg stat
sudo ceph health detail
Deliverable: Cluster health should return to HEALTH_OK (or remain there) within a few minutes of applying these settings, with no new stuck or inactive PGs. If PGs are peering or activating, wait and re-check with sudo ceph -s until the counts stabilize.

Task 2: Analyze and Optimize Network Throughput and Latency
Subtask 2.1: Measure Current Network Performance
Check network interface configuration on key nodes:
for node in ceph-mon-01 ceph-osd-01 ceph-osd-02 ceph-osd-03; do
    echo "=== $node ==="
    ssh $node "ip addr show | grep -E 'inet '"
    ssh $node "ethtool eth0 2>/dev/null | grep Speed"
done
Test latency between nodes:
cat > ~/test-latency.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

nodes=("ceph-mon-01" "ceph-osd-01" "ceph-osd-02" "ceph-osd-03" "ceph-mgr-01")

echo "Network Latency Test Results:"
echo "============================="

for source in "${nodes[@]}"; do
    for target in "${nodes[@]}"; do
        if [ "$source" != "$target" ]; then
            echo -n "$source -> $target: "
            ssh "$source" "ping -c 3 -q $target 2>/dev/null | tail -1 | awk -F'/' '{print \$5\" ms avg\"}'" || echo "Failed"
        fi
    done
done
EOF

chmod +x ~/test-latency.sh
./test-latency.sh | tee ~/baseline-latency.txt
Test throughput with iperf3:
# Start server on one OSD node
ssh ceph-osd-01 "iperf3 -s -D"

echo "Network Throughput Test Results:" | tee ~/baseline-throughput.txt
echo "===============================" | tee -a ~/baseline-throughput.txt
for node in ceph-osd-02 ceph-osd-03 ceph-mgr-01; do
    echo "Testing $node -> ceph-osd-01:" | tee -a ~/baseline-throughput.txt
    ssh $node "iperf3 -c ceph-osd-01 -t 10 -P 4" | tee -a ~/baseline-throughput.txt
    echo "" | tee -a ~/baseline-throughput.txt
done
Deliverable: Two files, ~/baseline-latency.txt and ~/baseline-throughput.txt, containing real measured round-trip times (ms) and bandwidth figures (Gbits/sec) between your cluster nodes. Note the average latency and average bandwidth â�� you will compare against these later.

Subtask 2.2: Apply Network and Messenger Optimizations
Apply kernel-level network buffer tuning on the monitor and OSD nodes:
cat > ~/optimize-network.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "Applying network optimizations..."

sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf > /dev/null << SYSCTL
net.core.rmem_max = 134217728
net.core.wmem_max = 134217728
net.core.rmem_default = 65536
net.core.wmem_default = 65536
net.ipv4.tcp_rmem = 4096 65536 134217728
net.ipv4.tcp_wmem = 4096 65536 134217728
net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max = 1048576
SYSCTL

sudo sysctl -p

echo "Network optimization complete"
EOF

chmod +x ~/optimize-network.sh

for node in ceph-mon-01 ceph-osd-01 ceph-osd-02 ceph-osd-03; do
    echo "Optimizing network on $node"
    scp ~/optimize-network.sh $node:~/
    ssh $node "bash ~/optimize-network.sh"
done
Apply Ceph messenger-level tuning:
sudo ceph config set global ms_tcp_nodelay true
sudo ceph config set global ms_tcp_rcvbuf 65536
sudo ceph config set global ms_cluster_mode crc
sudo ceph config set global ms_service_mode crc
Re-run the latency and throughput tests and compare:
./test-latency.sh | tee ~/after-latency.txt
diff ~/baseline-latency.txt ~/after-latency.txt || true
Deliverable: A diff output between baseline-latency.txt and after-latency.txt. Record whether average latency values decreased, stayed flat, or increased. In a lab environment with lightly loaded virtual networking, it is normal and acceptable to see little or no measurable change â�� the important outcome is that you can produce and read a real before/after comparison, not that a specific improvement threshold is hit.

Task 3: Identify and Mitigate Storage Hotspots
Subtask 3.1: Detect Hotspots
Review OSD utilization and PG distribution:
sudo ceph osd df
sudo ceph osd perf
sudo ceph pg dump osds | awk 'NR>1 {print $1, $2}' | sort -k2 -n
Create and run a dedicated hotspot detection script:
cat > ~/detect-hotspots.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "Ceph Storage Hotspot Analysis"
echo "============================"
echo "Timestamp: $(date)"
echo ""

echo "OSD Utilization Analysis:"
echo "------------------------"
sudo ceph osd df | awk 'NR>1 && $8 ~ /^[0-9.]+$/ {
    if ($8 > 1.2) print "HOT: OSD." $1 " - Usage: " $8 "x average"
    else if ($8 < 0.8) print "COLD: OSD." $1 " - Usage: " $8 "x average"
}'
echo ""

echo "PG Distribution Analysis:"
echo "------------------------"
sudo ceph osd df | awk 'NR>1 {print $1, $2}' | sort -k2 -n | awk '
{total+=$2; count++; osds[count]=$1; pgs[count]=$2}
END {
    if (count == 0) { print "No data"; exit }
    avg=total/count
    print "Average PGs per OSD: " avg
    print "Hotspot OSDs (>120% of average):"
    for(i=1; i<=count; i++) {
        if(pgs[i] > avg*1.2) print "  OSD." osds[i] ": " pgs[i] " PGs (" int(pgs[i]/avg*100) "% of average)"
    }
}'
echo ""

echo "Slow Request Analysis:"
echo "---------------------"
sudo ceph health detail | grep -i slow || echo "No slow requests detected"
EOF

chmod +x ~/detect-hotspots.sh
./detect-hotspots.sh
Deliverable: Console output listing any OSDs flagged HOT or COLD, and the count of pools/PGs that exceed 120 percent of the average. If no hotspots exist yet (likely on a freshly deployed lab cluster), note that explicitly â�� this is expected on a small, evenly-provisioned cluster.

Subtask 3.2: Mitigate Hotspots
Reweight OSDs based on utilization:
cat > ~/reweight-osds.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "OSD Reweighting for Hotspot Mitigation"
echo "======================================"

sudo ceph osd df | awk 'NR>1 && $8 ~ /^[0-9.]+$/ {
    osd=$1; weight=$3; util=$8
    if (util > 1.3) {
        new_weight = weight * 0.9
        print "sudo ceph osd reweight osd." osd " " new_weight " # Current util: " util
    } else if (util < 0.7) {
        new_weight = weight * 1.1
        if (new_weight > 1.0) new_weight = 1.0
        print "sudo ceph osd reweight osd." osd " " new_weight " # Current util: " util
    }
}' > /tmp/reweight_commands.sh

echo "Suggested reweighting commands:"
cat /tmp/reweight_commands.sh

if [ -s /tmp/reweight_commands.sh ]; then
    read -p "Apply these reweighting changes? (y/N): " confirm
    if [[ $confirm == [yY] ]]; then
        bash /tmp/reweight_commands.sh
        echo "Reweighting applied. Current status:"
        sudo ceph -s
    else
        echo "Reweighting cancelled."
    fi
else
    echo "No OSDs exceed the reweighting thresholds. No action needed."
fi
EOF

chmod +x ~/reweight-osds.sh
./reweight-osds.sh
Check and optimize CRUSH tunables:
sudo ceph osd crush show-tunables

read -p "Apply optimal CRUSH tunables? (y/N): " confirm
if [[ $confirm == [yY] ]]; then
    sudo ceph osd crush tunables optimal
    echo "Optimal tunables applied. This may trigger data movement."
    sudo ceph -s
fi
Deliverable: Either a log of reweight commands actually executed (with before/after ceph osd df showing utilization moving closer to the average), or, if no OSD needed reweighting, a one-line note in your own words confirming the cluster was already balanced.

Subtask 3.3: Validate Against the Baseline
Now compare your cluster's current state directly against the baseline captured in Task 0.

Re-run the hotspot monitor:
./hotspot-monitor.sh | tee ~/after-metrics.txt
Diff against the baseline you saved before making any changes:
diff ~/baseline-metrics.txt ~/after-metrics.txt || true
Create a comparison script that reads both real files rather than hardcoded numbers:
cat > ~/performance-comparison.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

BASELINE=~/baseline-metrics.txt
CURRENT=~/after-metrics.txt

if [ ! -f "$BASELINE" ]; then
    echo "ERROR: Baseline file $BASELINE not found."
    echo "Run Task 0 (./hotspot-monitor.sh | tee ~/baseline-metrics.txt) before comparing."
    exit 1
fi

if [ ! -f "$CURRENT" ]; then
    echo "Generating current metrics snapshot..."
    ./hotspot-monitor.sh | tee "$CURRENT"
fi

echo "Performance Optimization Results"
echo "==============================="
echo ""
echo "Baseline metrics (captured before optimization):"
echo "-------------------------------------------------"
grep -E "Coefficient of variation|Average PGs per OSD|WARNING|No slow operations" "$BASELINE"
echo ""
echo "Current metrics (after optimization):"
echo "--------------------------------------"
grep -E "Coefficient of variation|Average PGs per OSD|WARNING|No slow operations" "$CURRENT"
echo ""
echo "Full diff:"
echo "----------"
diff "$BASELINE" "$CURRENT" || echo "(no textual differences detected)"
EOF

chmod +x ~/performance-comparison.sh
./performance-comparison.sh
Deliverable: Output of performance-comparison.sh showing the baseline coefficient of variation, PG distribution status, and slow-op count side by side with the current values, plus the full diff. Every number in this report must trace back to a file your commands actually generated â�� there should be no hardcoded "before" values anywhere in this script.

Task 4: Validate Performance with RADOS Bench
Subtask 4.1: Run Benchmark Tests
cat > ~/performance-test.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "Ceph Performance Validation Test"
echo "==============================="

sudo ceph osd pool create benchmark 128 128
sudo ceph osd pool application enable benchmark rbd

echo "1. Sequential Write Test (10 seconds):"
sudo rados bench -p benchmark 10 write --no-cleanup | grep -E "bandwidth|Average Latency"
echo ""

echo "2. Sequential Read Test (10 seconds):"
sudo rados bench -p benchmark 10 seq | grep -E "bandwidth|Average Latency"
echo ""

echo "3. Random Read Test (10 seconds):"
sudo rados bench -p benchmark 10 rand | grep -E "bandwidth|Average Latency"
echo ""

echo "Cleaning up test objects..."
sudo rados -p benchmark cleanup

sudo ceph osd pool delete benchmark benchmark --yes-i-really-really-mean-it

echo "Performance test completed."
EOF

chmod +x ~/performance-test.sh
./performance-test.sh | tee ~/rados-bench-results.txt
Deliverable: ~/rados-bench-results.txt containing actual measured bandwidth (MB/s) and average latency (seconds) for write, sequential read, and random read tests. Identify which operation type had the highest and lowest bandwidth in your results.

Expected Outcomes
A documented, measured baseline (baseline-metrics.txt, baseline-latency.txt, baseline-throughput.txt) captured before any tuning was applied
CRUSH map and OSD parameters tuned for improved I/O scheduling, recovery throttling, and cache usage, verified through ceph config dump
A final, evidence-based comparison (performance-comparison.sh output and RADOS bench results) showing real before/after cluster metrics rather than assumed values
Conclusion
In this lab, you worked through a complete performance optimization cycle on a live Ceph cluster: capturing a real baseline, tuning CRUSH placement rules and OSD parameters, adjusting network and messenger settings, detecting and mitigating storage hotspots, and validating every change against the data you actually measured rather than assumed figures. You built reusable diagnostic tooling â�� hotspot-monitor.sh, detect-hotspots.sh, reweight-osds.sh, and performance-comparison.sh â�� that reads live cluster state on every run, so the before/after comparisons you produced reflect genuine cluster behavior. By the end of the lab you should be able to explain, with your own measured numbers, whether your optimizations reduced utilization variance, improved PG balance, or changed network latency and RADOS bench throughput, and you should be comfortable applying this same measure-tune-validate workflow to a production Ceph cluster.
